\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Eliminating a chosen summand}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{When some field values can be solved for, which equation remains
on the others? A [chosen partition](fdip-001I) gives one splitting
of a field module. More generally, a specified split module and an
invertible eliminated component determine a retained linear map.
[Triangular factorization](fdip-001U) explains its
[inverse](fdip-001V) and [kernel](fdip-001W) before
any pairing or probability law is chosen.}

\p{[Perfect symmetric pairings](fdip-001O) turn this factorization
into a [congruence](fdip-001X) when the splitting is orthogonal.
Positive real inner products then give the
[Schur positivity criterion](fdip-001Y), and
[cycle elimination](fdip-001Z) makes the retained inverse comparison explicit.
The later [Gaussian elimination](fdip-0018) adds the measure and
normalization needed to interpret a retained marginal.}

\transclude{fdip-001T}
\transclude{fdip-001U}
\transclude{fdip-001V}
\transclude{fdip-001W}
\transclude{fdip-001X}
\transclude{fdip-001Y}
\transclude{fdip-001Z}
